        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Paver Sealer Not Drying",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/paver-sealer-not-drying/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is my paver sealer still tacky?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Almost always one of three things: applied too thick so the film skinned over and trapped its carrier, applied too cold so a water-based film never coalesced, or moisture in the pavers from washing, rain, dew or irrigation. Thickness is the most common by a wide margin."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should paver sealer take to dry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Touch-dry in one to four hours and walkable at about 24 in good conditions, meaning around 20 degrees with moderate humidity and airflow. A shaded, enclosed, humid patio at 12 degrees can legitimately stay tacky for two days with nothing wrong. Judge against your conditions, not a general figure."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will tacky paver sealer eventually dry?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often yes, if the cause is excess thickness or trapped moisture — given warmth, airflow and one to three weeks. It will not if a water-based film was applied too cold to coalesce, because the particles dried without fusing and warmth afterwards cannot reverse that. That case needs removal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I put another coat on to fix tacky sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No, and it is the most damaging common response. The film is soft precisely because its carrier cannot escape, and adding more film on top removes what little chance it had. This turns a problem that might have resolved itself into one that requires stripping."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I fix sticky solvent-based paver sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Xylene. Applying it re-dissolves the film and opens it so trapped solvent or moisture can escape, after which it re-cures properly. It is the standard professional rescue and works only on solvent-based acrylic, not water-based. Test a small area first and take the fume hazard seriously."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does cold weather make paver sealer stay tacky?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and the distinction matters. Cold that merely slows a cure is recoverable with warmth. Cold below about 10 degrees that prevented a water-based film from coalescing is not — the acrylic particles dried without fusing, leaving a chalky weak layer that warmth cannot fix afterwards."
          }
        },
