          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Resist the urge to do anything for the first few days. A meaningful share of tacky patios resolve themselves given warmth and airflow, and the interventions people reach for first tend to make the recoverable cases unrecoverable.
        </p>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Is Too Long?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before diagnosing a problem, check whether you have one. Expectations here are often wrong in both directions.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time Since Application</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Still Tacky?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wait</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4-12 hours, cool or humid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wait</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24 hours, good conditions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concerning</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Start diagnosing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">48 hours, good conditions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A problem</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diagnose and act</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">72 hours+, any conditions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A problem</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Act</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A week, warm and dry throughout</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Serious</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Likely needs removal</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Conditions extend all of these considerably. A shaded, enclosed, humid patio at 12 degrees can legitimately stay tacky for two days with nothing wrong, where the same product on an open terrace at 22 degrees would be walkable in twelve hours. Judge against your actual conditions, not a general figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distinguish tacky from soft, too. A film that feels slightly grippy underfoot but does not mark is curing normally and slowly. A film that takes a fingerprint, transfers to your shoe, or picks up grit is genuinely uncured and needs attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing you can do at 24 hours is nothing, provided the weather is improving. Warmth and airflow solve a substantial share of these cases, and every intervention available makes the situation harder to recover if applied too early.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">FIRST RESPONSE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Give it three warm, dry, breezy days before doing anything.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Most tacky patios are slow rather than failed, and warmth plus air movement resolves them. The interventions people reach for at 24 hours — washing it, adding another coat, scrubbing it — turn a recoverable slow cure into a genuine removal job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="too-thick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Applied Too Thick: The Overwhelming Cause
          </h2>

          <p>
            If a patio is still tacky at 48 hours in decent weather, this is the first and most likely explanation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealer cures from the surface downward. A heavy film skins over on top while the material underneath still holds its carrier, and that skin then prevents the rest escaping. The surface can feel dry while a layer beneath remains liquid, and the whole film stays soft indefinitely because the carrier has nowhere to go.
          </p>

          <p>
            It happens in predictable places rather than uniformly across a patio. Low spots where the product pooled. The edges of each pass where overlap doubled the thickness. Areas worked twice because a spot looked thin. Around the joints where sealer ran in and collected. If the tackiness follows a pattern rather than covering the whole patio evenly, thickness is almost certainly the cause.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also why two thin coats is the constant advice and why people ignore it. A single generous coat looks more efficient and produces exactly this outcome, along with the blushing covered in our guide on why paver sealer turns white, which is the same mechanism showing a different symptom.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that thickness-related tackiness often does resolve, given enough time, warmth and air movement. It can take one to three weeks rather than days, which tests patience, but the film does eventually release its carrier and harden. Warm dry weather is the whole treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bad news is that a genuinely excessive film may never fully harden, and remains soft enough to hold dirt and mark permanently. That is the case that needs removal, and it is usually obvious because the surface stays visibly wet-looking and gummy rather than merely grippy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Too Cold to Cure
          </h2>

          <p>
            The second most common cause, and the one most likely to be a permanent problem rather than a slow one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based sealers need to reach a minimum film formation temperature for the acrylic particles to coalesce into a continuous film. Below it they never fuse, and the result is not a slow cure but a failed one — a chalky, weak layer that stays soft and powders rather than hardening. Warmth afterwards does not reverse it, because the particles have already dried in place without fusing.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the crucial distinction. Cold that slows a cure is recoverable; cold that prevented coalescence is not. If your patio was sealed below about 10 degrees Celsius, or the overnight temperature dropped well below that before the film set, expect the latter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based sealers in cold are usually just slow rather than failed, because there is no coalescence step. They will generally come good with warmth, though it can take weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The overnight temperature is the variable people miss. A film applied at a pleasant 18 degrees that then sits through a 4-degree night has not finished forming when it crosses the threshold, which is why our guide on the best temperature to seal pavers puts so much emphasis on checking tonight's low rather than today's high.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diagnostically: a cold-failed water-based film feels chalky or powdery rather than gummy, may rub off on a finger as dust rather than as sticky residue, and is usually uniform across the patio rather than patchy. A thickness problem is gummy and patchy; a cold failure is chalky and even.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Moisture in the Pavers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The third major cause, and the one that most often produces both tackiness and the white blushing at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers are porous and hold water well after the surface looks and feels dry. Seal over that and the moisture is trapped beneath the film, where it interferes with curing and pushes back against the material. The result is a soft film, often with milky patches, that will not harden until the moisture finds its way out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual culprits are sealing too soon after washing, rain within the curing window, dew on a clear cool night, and an irrigation zone that runs onto the patio in the early morning. The sprinkler case is extraordinarily common and entirely invisible in the forecast.
          </p>

          <p>
            New pavers are a special case. Fresh concrete continues to release moisture and soluble salts for months, and sealing within 60 to 90 days traps both. The tackiness usually resolves; the white efflorescence bloom under the film usually does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The diagnosis is contextual rather than physical: think back to what the pavers experienced in the 48 hours before and after sealing. If there was washing, rain, heavy dew or irrigation in that window, moisture is the likely cause whatever else is also true.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture cases have the best prognosis of the three. Given warm dry weather and airflow, the water does eventually escape and the film hardens. A solvent-based film can also be re-opened with xylene to release it deliberately, which is covered below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-causes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Other Causes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Less common but worth ruling out, particularly if the three main causes do not fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Contamination Under the Sealer</h3>

          <p>
            Oil, grease, silicone or previous sealer residue on the pavers prevents proper adhesion and can leave the film soft in those areas. Usually localised rather than patio-wide, and often follows a pattern that matches where a car parked or a barbecue stood.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stripper residue is the version of this that follows a stripping job. Insufficient rinsing after removing an old sealer leaves chemical in the surface that interferes with the new film, which our guide on removing sealer from pavers warns about specifically.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Incompatible Products</h3>

          <p>
            Water-based applied over an existing solvent-based film, or two incompatible chemistries layered, can produce a film that never bonds or cures properly. If you sealed over an existing coating without knowing what it was, this moves up the list considerably.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old or Badly Stored Product</h3>

          <p>
            Sealer that has frozen in a garage over winter, or that is years past its date, can be compromised. Water-based emulsions in particular are damaged by freezing and may never cure properly afterwards. Check the tin for a date and think about where it has been stored.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over-Application of a Second Coat</h3>

          <p>
            Applying the second coat before the first had released its carrier traps everything underneath. Respecting the recoat window on the tin matters in both directions, and too soon is the more damaging error.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Actually Fixes It
          </h2>

          <p>
            In order, from least to most invasive. Work down the list rather than jumping to the end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wait, with warmth and airflow. Three to seven warm dry days with a breeze resolves a large share of cases. If you can increase air movement across the patio with a fan, do; if the weather is due to improve, wait for it. This costs nothing and forecloses nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For solvent-based films specifically, xylene. Applying xylene re-dissolves the film, opening it so trapped solvent or moisture can escape, after which it re-cures properly. This is a genuine rescue rather than a patch, it is the standard professional response, and it works only on solvent-based acrylic. Test a small area first, follow the product guidance, and treat the fume hazard seriously.
          </p>

          <p>
            Absorbing excess on a genuinely over-thick film. On a patio where sealer pooled, spreading dry sawdust or an absorbent granule over the worst areas and brushing it off can lift some of the surplus, which then lets the remainder cure. Crude, occasionally effective, and best on localised puddles rather than a whole patio.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stripping, for the cases the above cannot reach. A cold-failed water-based film that never coalesced, a contaminated area, an incompatible layering, or a film so thick it will not release — these need removal. Our guide on removing sealer from pavers covers what that involves, and it is a substantial job.
          </p>

          <p>
            What not to do: do not apply another coat hoping to seal over the tackiness, which adds film to a film that already cannot release its carrier. Do not wash or pressure wash it, which adds moisture to the exact problem. And do not scrub it, which embeds grit into a soft film permanently.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Prognosis by Cause</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Good</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Too thick — usually cures, slowly</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Good</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Moisture — escapes with warmth</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Poor</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cold-failed water-based film</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Poor</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Contamination or incompatibility</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Preventing a Repeat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whatever you end up doing to fix it, the next application should not repeat it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two thin coats, never one heavy one. This single rule prevents the most common cause outright, and it produces a better-looking, more durable result as well. If a coat looks thin while you are applying it, that is what a correct coat looks like.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check surface temperature rather than air temperature, and check the overnight low. An infrared thermometer costs very little and removes the largest source of error. Our guide on the best temperature to seal pavers covers the window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confirm the pavers are dry through with the taped plastic square test — a foot square of clear plastic sealed on all four edges for a few hours, with condensation underneath meaning moisture is still coming out. This takes an afternoon and prevents the moisture case entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Turn the irrigation off for several days before and after, and check which zones actually reach the patio rather than assuming. This is the most preventable cause on the list and it catches people repeatedly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy fresh product and store it somewhere that does not freeze. A water-based emulsion that has frozen may never cure properly, and the tin gives no outward sign.
          </p>

          <p>
            And do a test patch with a new product or on an unfamiliar surface. A square foot, left for several days, tells you how this specific sealer behaves on these specific pavers in this weather — which is more useful than any general guidance including this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Acting at 24 Hours Instead of Waiting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A meaningful share of tacky patios are curing slowly rather than failing, and three to seven warm dry breezy days resolves them. Every intervention available — washing it, recoating it, scrubbing it — turns a recoverable slow cure into a genuine removal job. Do nothing first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Another Coat to Seal Over the Tackiness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The film is soft because its carrier cannot escape. Adding more film on top gives the trapped carrier even less chance of leaving and guarantees the problem becomes permanent. This is the most common instinct and the most reliably damaging response.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing or Pressure Washing a Tacky Film</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If moisture is the cause, you are adding more of exactly what is preventing the cure. If thickness is the cause, water on a soft film embeds dirt and grit permanently. Neither version helps, and pressure washing a soft film also tears it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Product Was Defective</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nine times out of ten the cause is application thickness, temperature or moisture in the pavers. Defective sealer does exist — particularly a water-based emulsion that has frozen in storage — but it is far down the list, and diagnosing properly determines whether the patio recovers on its own.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing New Pavers Within 60 to 90 Days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh concrete releases moisture and soluble salts for months. Sealing early traps both, producing a soft film and a white efflorescence bloom underneath. The tackiness usually resolves eventually; the bloom under the film generally does not, and that is a stripping job.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my paver sealer still tacky?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always one of three things: applied too thick so the film skinned over and trapped its carrier, applied too cold so a water-based film never coalesced, or moisture in the pavers from washing, rain, dew or irrigation. Thickness is the most common by a wide margin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should paver sealer take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Touch-dry in one to four hours and walkable at about 24 in good conditions, meaning around 20 degrees with moderate humidity and airflow. A shaded, enclosed, humid patio at 12 degrees can legitimately stay tacky for two days with nothing wrong. Judge against your conditions, not a general figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will tacky paver sealer eventually dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, if the cause is excess thickness or trapped moisture — given warmth, airflow and one to three weeks. It will not if a water-based film was applied too cold to coalesce, because the particles dried without fusing and warmth afterwards cannot reverse that. That case needs removal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put another coat on to fix tacky sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and it is the most damaging common response. The film is soft precisely because its carrier cannot escape, and adding more film on top removes what little chance it had. This turns a problem that might have resolved itself into one that requires stripping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I fix sticky solvent-based paver sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Xylene. Applying it re-dissolves the film and opens it so trapped solvent or moisture can escape, after which it re-cures properly. It is the standard professional rescue and works only on solvent-based acrylic, not water-based. Test a small area first and take the fume hazard seriously.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does cold weather make paver sealer stay tacky?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and the distinction matters. Cold that merely slows a cure is recoverable with warmth. Cold below about 10 degrees that prevented a water-based film from coalescing is not — the acrylic particles dried without fusing, leaving a chalky weak layer that warmth cannot fix afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I tell if it is too thick or too cold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A thickness problem is gummy and patchy, following low spots, overlaps and areas worked twice. A cold failure is chalky or powdery and fairly uniform across the whole patio, and may rub off as dust rather than sticky residue. Moisture problems often show white blushing alongside the tackiness.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

