        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do nothing for three to seven warm, dry, breezy days. A large share of tacky patios are curing slowly rather than failing, and warmth plus airflow resolves them — while every intervention people reach for at 24 hours makes a recoverable case unrecoverable. Adding another coat is the most damaging of them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then diagnose by pattern. Gummy and patchy, following low spots and overlaps, means too thick, and that usually comes good eventually. Chalky and uniform means a water-based film applied too cold to coalesce, which warmth cannot fix and which needs removal. White blushing alongside the tackiness points at moisture in the pavers, which escapes given time — and on a solvent-based film, xylene will let it out deliberately.
          </p>
        </div>

